      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Theoretically, sanctions can pressure regimens to adopt democratic reforms by increasing the political cost of authoritarian rule, yet empirical evidence often shows limited success in consistent outcomes. Many scholars argue that these measures disproportionately harm civilian populations and allow incumbent leaders to deflect blame upon foreign aggressors, effectively serving as coercive bullying for powerful nations. A strong thesis might assert that while sanctions are a necessary part of the I.S. toolkit, their efficacy depends entirely on the specific political context and the coordinated support of international institutions.",
